    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Reading a syllable",
     "texto": [
      "A syllable usually has an initial consonant, a vowel and, sometimes, a final consonant. When reading **กิน**, the vowel **ิ** goes with ก, and น closes the syllable: it is pronounced roughly «kin». In Thai writing, the words in a sentence are usually not all separated by spaces; here we add spaces between words so that you can recognise them, and so that the app's checker works."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
